-- title:
--   Eichler–Shimura relation: sp^{Pic^0} intertwines T_ℓ with the geometric Hecke correspondence
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$, and assume `HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar N`, i.e. the operators `heckeOperatorBar N` attached to any two primes commute on $J_0(N) := \mathrm{Pic}^0_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar N`. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $\ell$ in the sense that the image of $\ell$ is a non-unit of $A$, and write $k$ for the residue field of $A$; assume $k$ has characteristic $\ell$, is algebraically closed, and that `modularFunctionFieldC k N` (the subfield of $k$-Laurent series generated by the reductions of $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$) is a curve over $k$, i.e. has principal divisors of degree zero, finite residue extensions, and free rank-one module of Kähler differentials. Let `data` consist of a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(\ell)$ annihilating $j(q^\ell)$ over $j$, subject to the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \equiv (X^\ell - Y)(X - Y^\ell) \bmod \ell$, and assume the two ring homomorphisms `heckeAlphaBar`, `heckeBetaBar` at level $(N,\ell)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are integral. Then for every place-specialization packet $S$ for these data, with reduction map $A \to k$, the additive map $S.\mathrm{spPic0} : J_0(N) \to \mathrm{Pic}^0_k(\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC } k\ N)$ satisfies, for all $x \in J_0(N)$, $\bar T_\ell(S.\mathrm{spPic0}\,x) = S.\mathrm{spPic0}(X_\ell \cdot x)$, where $\bar T_\ell$ is `heckeFibreGeomLevelPic0OfIsCurveOver` and $X_\ell$ is the generator `heckeGen` of the Hecke algebra acting through `heckeModuleBar N`.
--
--   This is the Eichler–Shimura congruence relation in the form needed downstream: the specialization of $J_0(N)$ to the fibre at $\ell$ carries the Hecke operator $T_\ell$ to the geometric Hecke correspondence in characteristic $\ell$ coming from the Kronecker congruence. It feeds the construction of Hecke-equivariant descent families and glued specializations used in the level-lowering argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_spPic0_heckeGen_ell_eq_heckeFibreGeom.lean

